---
// ===========================================================================
// runtime.astro — what a write touches, and when a verdict happens.
//
// Every figure on this page came from running the thing, not from reading it:
// a counting adapter around the real resolver, and subscription counters on
// three cells. The tables are what it printed.
// ===========================================================================
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import Code from "../components/Code.astro";

const writing = `
// Every write goes through a handle. There is no setState, no form object to
// spread, and no path string that only the runtime understands.
form.field("owner.name").setValue("Grace");
form.field("items[0].sku").setValue("A-1");

form.rows("items").insert(2, { sku: "" });
form.rows("items").remove(0);
form.rows("items").move(0, 2);

form.reset();                    // back to the defaults it was built with
form.reset(otherDefaults);       // or to different ones
`;

const coalesced = `
// One event handler, three writes.
function applyPreset() {
  form.field("owner.name").setValue("Grace");
  form.field("owner.nick").setValue("grace");
  form.field("items[0].sku").setValue("A-1");
}
// -> 0 validation passes so far.
// -> 1 pass, one microtask later: after the handler returns, before paint.
`;

const asyncOne = `
// Passes are numbered. Type, wait, type again, and the slow answer to the
// first one arrives after the second has been committed.
field.setValue("Ad");    // pass 1 leaves, asking the server
field.setValue("Ada");   // pass 2 leaves
// pass 2 answers  -> committed
// pass 1 answers  -> DROPPED. It describes a root that is no longer there.
`;

const submitting = `
const outcome = await form.submit((root) => save(root));

outcome.submitted   // false when anything blocked
outcome.blockedBy   // every blocking issue, including paths with no component
`;

// Measured with a counting adapter wrapped around the real zod resolver.
const triggers = [
  ["field.setValue(next)", "1", "Coalesced: many writes in one turn are one pass."],
  ["field.markTouched()", "0", "Touched is display state. Nothing to re-judge."],
  ["rows.insert / remove / move", "1", "Each edit changes the root."],
  ["field.setParticipating(b)", "1", "What blocks a submit changed."],
  ["form.reset(...)", "1", "The new root has not been judged yet."],
  ["form.validate()", "1", "Immediately, not on the microtask."],
  ["field.issuesFor(candidate)", "1", "On a probe root. Writes nothing at all."],
  ["form.submit(handler)", "1", "Immediately, before the handler is considered."],
  ["createForm(...)", "0", "Nothing is judged until something asks."],
];

const woken = [
  ["Writing the same value again", "nobody", "The store gates an Object.is-equal write."],
  [
    "A different value, same verdict",
    "that field's value",
    "Its issues cell is not written, so an error display beside it does not re-render.",
  ],
  [
    "A value that becomes invalid",
    "that field's value and issues",
    "The pass judged four hundred fields; it woke the one whose error moved.",
  ],
  ["Any of the above", "never another field", "Unless a rule actually moved that field's issues."],
];
---

<Base
  title="The runtime — form-contract"
  description="How a value is written, what one write touches, and exactly when validation fires. One coalesced whole-root pass per settled change, with the verdict scattered back only onto the paths whose issues actually moved."
>
  <h1 class="text-3xl font-bold sm:text-4xl">How a value changes</h1>

  <p class="mt-6 leading-relaxed text-on-surface-variant">
    Values live in a flat, path-keyed cell space outside React. A component
    does not own a value and does not lift one: it subscribes to the cells it
    reads, and writes through a handle addressed by path.
  </p>

  <Code code={writing} />

  <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-on-surface-variant">
    One <code>setValue</code> writes the root, the leaf's own cell, its dirty
    flag — and of its containers, only the ones somebody is currently reading.
    A leaf edit with no container on screen touches three cells, whatever the
    depth of the path.
  </p>

  <h2 class="mt-16 text-2xl font-bold">Validation fires once per settled change</h2>
  <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-on-surface-variant">
    A single user action is many writes, and judging on each of them would run
    the whole schema several times for one keystroke. Requests are coalesced
    into <strong class="text-on-surface">one microtask</strong>: the pass runs
    after the event handler has returned and before the browser paints, so
    nothing renders twice and no verdict is a frame late.
  </p>

  <Code code={coalesced} />

  <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-on-surface-variant">
    The microtask comes from a resolved promise rather than{" "}
    <code>queueMicrotask</code>, which is a host global — this runtime depends
    on the language and not on an environment, because it runs wherever a
    validator does.
  </p>

  <h2 class="mt-16 text-2xl font-bold">What asks for a pass, and what does not</h2>

  <div class="mt-6 overflow-x-auto">
    <table class="w-full text-sm">
      <thead class="text-left text-on-surface-variant">
        <tr class="hairline">
          <th class="py-2 pr-4 font-medium">Call</th>
          <th class="py-2 pr-4 font-medium">Passes</th>
          <th class="py-2 font-medium">Why</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {
          triggers.map(([call, n, why]) => (
            <tr class="hairline">
              <td class="py-3 pr-4 align-top font-mono text-xs whitespace-nowrap">
                {call}
              </td>
              <td class="py-3 pr-4 align-top font-mono text-xs tabular-nums">{n}</td>
              <td class="py-3 align-top leading-relaxed text-on-surface-variant">
                {why}
              </td>
            </tr>
          ))
        }
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p class="mt-6 rounded-lg border border-outline-variant p-5 leading-relaxed">
    <strong>A new form has not been judged.</strong> Build one from defaults
    that violate the schema and <code>errorCount</code> is still{" "}
    <code>0</code> — not because the form is clean, but because nothing has
    asked yet. Call <code>form.validate()</code> if a screen should open
    showing its errors.
  </p>

  <h2 class="mt-16 text-2xl font-bold">Who gets woken</h2>
  <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-on-surface-variant">
    Computation is proportional to the schema; notification is proportional to
    what actually moved. Paths that <em>had</em> issues are revisited as well as
    paths that have them now, so a fixed error is cleared rather than left on
    screen.
  </p>

  <div class="mt-6 overflow-x-auto">
    <table class="w-full text-sm">
      <thead class="text-left text-on-surface-variant">
        <tr class="hairline">
          <th class="py-2 pr-4 font-medium">The write</th>
          <th class="py-2 pr-4 font-medium">Woken</th>
          <th class="py-2 font-medium">Note</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {
          woken.map(([what, who, note]) => (
            <tr class="hairline">
              <td class="py-3 pr-4 align-top leading-relaxed">{what}</td>
              <td class="py-3 pr-4 align-top font-mono text-xs">{who}</td>
              <td class="py-3 align-top leading-relaxed text-on-surface-variant">
                {note}
              </td>
            </tr>
          ))
        }
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2 class="mt-16 text-2xl font-bold">A validator that has to ask something</h2>
  <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-on-surface-variant">
    <code>validate</code> may return a promise, so a rule can ask a server
    whether a handle is taken. While any pass is in flight{" "}
    <code>form.validating</code> is <code>true</code>.
  </p>

  <Code code={asyncOne} />

  <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-on-surface-variant">
    A late answer is dropped rather than written — otherwise typing during a
    slow check would flicker the form back to the verdict for what was typed
    before. A pass that <em>throws</em> leaves the previous verdict standing: an
    error reaching the network is not evidence that the form became acceptable.
  </p>

  <h2 class="mt-16 text-2xl font-bold">Submitting</h2>
  <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-on-surface-variant">
    Submit judges the whole root first, so a field nobody has mounted is judged
    too. A form that submits because the offending input was off screen is the
    defect this exists to prevent.
  </p>

  <Code code={submitting} />

  <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-on-surface-variant">
    What blocks is reported rather than counted, and <code>blockedBy</code>{" "}
    carries the path — so an application can open the right section instead of
    showing a disabled button with no explanation. The handler runs only when
    nothing blocks, and a handler that throws leaves the form not submitted
    rather than half submitted.
  </p>

  <h2 class="mt-16 text-2xl font-bold">Asking without writing</h2>
  <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-on-surface-variant">
    <code>field.issuesFor(candidate)</code> answers for a value that is{" "}
    <em>not</em> in the form, and writes nothing — no value, no issue cell, no cross-field
    verdict. Asking whether a value would be acceptable is not the same as
    putting it in the form, and a probe that mutated the store would move every
    other field's verdict as a side effect of the question.
  </p>

  <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-on-surface-variant">
    <code>field.validate()</code> is the opposite: it is the form's own pass,
    filtered to that path, so a conditionally rendered field cannot get a
    different answer from a second engine.
  </p>

  <p class="mt-10 text-sm leading-relaxed text-on-surface-variant">
    Every number on this page was produced by running the runtime with a
    counting adapter wrapped around the real zod resolver, and subscription
    counters on the cells — not by reading the source.
  </p>
</Base>
